description:
Reported by user (2026-05-10): clicking Key/BPM/Find-similar in <ProjectInfo /> logs the axis to console + closes the inspector + lands on LibraryHome — but no chip and no filtering. The pivot appears to do nothing user-visible.

## Root cause

LibraryHome.tsx:44-48 attaches the 'clear filter on folder switch' behaviour to a useEffect cleanup keyed on folder.path:

```ts
useEffect(() => {
  return () => {
    useUIStore.getState().setLibrarySimilarityFilter(null);
  };
}, [folder.path]);
```

React.StrictMode (enabled in src/main.tsx:16) runs setup → cleanup → setup on the initial mount of every component to surface cleanup bugs. So:

1. Pivot click sets filter to e.g. 'C major' + selects containing folder.
2. App renders <LibraryHome /> for the first time.
3. StrictMode: setup runs (registers cleanup).
4. StrictMode: CLEANUP runs immediately — calls setLibrarySimilarityFilter(null), wiping the filter we just set.
5. StrictMode: setup runs again.
6. LibraryHome renders with filter === null → no chip, no filter applied.

## Fix

Track the previous folder.path with useRef. Only clear the filter when the path *changes* from one non-null value to a different non-null value — i.e., a genuine folder switch. Drop the cleanup-on-unmount entirely (or keep it, but separate from the on-mount-cleanup pattern that StrictMode breaks).

```ts
const prevFolderPathRef = useRef<string | null>(null);
useEffect(() => {
  if (
    prevFolderPathRef.current !== null &&
    prevFolderPathRef.current !== folder.path
  ) {
    useUIStore.getState().setLibrarySimilarityFilter(null);
  }
  prevFolderPathRef.current = folder.path;
}, [folder.path]);
```

## Test that would have caught this

Render <LibraryHome /> for the first time with a similarity filter pre-set in ui-store, AND <main.tsx>'s StrictMode behaviour (or simulate by manually calling effect cleanup before assertions). Assert filter is still set after first render. Existing tests passed because they don't run under the StrictMode setup-cleanup-setup cycle for fresh mounts.

Discovered in the lpx-explorer-bul follow-up review. TrackRow renders insert names via `labelOf(au)`:

```ts
function labelOf(au: AURef): string {
  return au.display_name ?? fingerprintOf(au);
}
```

It only checks the parser-set `display_name` (Apple stock plug-ins). For 3rd-party AUs without display_name (Scaler 2, EZkeys, etc.), it falls back to the raw fingerprint string, e.g. `aumi/S2lc/eMai` — even though `auval -l` clearly registers that fingerprint as 'Plugin Boutique: ScalerControl 2'.

PluginRail (PluginRail.tsx:66-72) does the lookup correctly — TrackRow should match.

## Fix

`TrackRow` already builds `byFingerprint: ReadonlyMap<string, AuvalEntry>`. Pass it (or the lookup) into `labelOf` and fall back to the raw fingerprint only when the registry has no entry.

```ts
function labelOf(au: AURef, byFingerprint: ReadonlyMap<string, AuvalEntry>): string {
  if (au.display_name !== undefined) return au.display_name;
  const fp = fingerprintOf(au);
  return byFingerprint.get(fp)?.name ?? fp;
}
```

## Note

This is bundled into the table-redesign bead's scope (lpx-explorer-XXX). Filing as its own bead so the bug is tracked even if the table redesign is deferred.

## Acceptance

- 3rd-party plug-ins inside a TrackRow disclosure render their auval registry name (e.g. 'Plugin Boutique: ScalerControl 2') when the registry contains an entry.
- Test: render TrackRow with an insert whose fingerprint is in the registry; assert the registry name is shown, not the fingerprint.

## Source

Verified against `auval -l` 2026-05-06 — `aumi S2lc eMai` resolves to 'Plugin Boutique: ScalerControl 2'.
